    $( Lemma for ~ iseralt .  From ~ iseraltlem2 , we have
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k ) <_ ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) ` and
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 1 ) <_ ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k + 1 ) ` ,
       and we also have ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 1 ) = `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) - G ( n + 1 ) ` for each ` n ` by the
       definition of the partial sum ` S ` , so combining the inequalities we
       get ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) - G ( n + 1 ) = `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 1 ) <_ `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k + 1 ) = `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k ) - G ( n + 2 k + 1 ) <_ `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k ) <_ ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) <_ `
       ` ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) + G ( n + 1 ) ` , so
       ` | ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k + 1 ) - ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) | = `
       ` | S ( n + 2 k + 1 ) - S ( n ) | <_ G ( n + 1 ) ` and
       ` | ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n + 2 k ) - ( -u 1 ^ n ) x. S ( n ) | = `
       ` | S ( n + 2 k ) - S ( n ) | <_ G ( n + 1 ) ` .  Thus, both even and
       odd partial sums are Cauchy if ` G ` converges to ` 0 ` .  (Contributed
       by Mario Carneiro, 6-Apr-2015.) $)
